Running the built plugin and application on a server you operate is not a
restricted act - that is what they are for.

A paid licence

A licence is bought for a one-off fee (US$50 at the time of writing). That fee
buys a lifetime licence: the person who paid may install and run the built
application for as long as they like, with no subscription, no renewal and no
expiry. The licence is for the buyer alone and is not transferable, and it
grants no right in the source.

Update requests are honoured for a limited period after purchase. "Limited"
means a window and not an open-ended commitment: while the window is open the
buyer may ask for fixes and improvements, and once it closes the version the
buyer already holds keeps working exactly as it does today. The length of that
window is set out on the product page when the licence is bought.

Third-party material

No third-party code, jar, asset or configuration file is bundled in, linked
into, or redistributed with PlayPort. Every line of the plugin, the panel and
the browser UI is written for this project. The third-party products PlayPort
interoperates with - server plugins it detects, imports from or speaks the
protocol of, and the server software and plugins it downloads at your request -
are used as published by their own authors under their own licences, and
PlayPort ships none of their code. Those downloads happen at your request, from
the vendor's own release channel.

Third-party names

Where another product's name appears in a shipped artifact, the name is a fact
about interoperability, not a claim about its code. The full list, with the
reason for each appearance, is kept by the copyright holder and is available on
request.

